00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  good afternoon and it's great to see all  of you here yes I'm a novice novice at  the cop that's true not so much actually  on unsustainability because in the  previous Commission in the Younger  Commission I held the responsibility for  sustainability for five years and  thinking back to be perfectly honest  with you when we started as younger  Commission sustainability was already a  big issue but not at the highest  political level because when creating  that Commission it was simply overlooked  and when the European Parliament  criticised jean-claude Juncker for this  I happen to be sitting next to him and  he looked at me he said well France  every month will be responsible for  sustainability and it's one of the  nicest things that happened to me  frankly so I want to to share with you a  couple of lessons I learned very briefly  and why it is important to take a more  holistic view of the issue first of all  we are in the middle of arguably the  biggest change the planet has ever seen  as long as humans are on it and I always  tell my kids you know I begin with this  remark we can't do without the planet  but the planet can very do without  without house it did without us for a  long long time keep that in mind  and if we don't behave on the planet the  planet that sooner or later is going to  get rid of us because we do too much  harm and it's a very direct way of  explaining to my kids what our  responsibility is and I say this because  we have a tendency to compartmentalize  the issues we talk about emissions and  that we have a climate crisis which is  linked to those emissions  and then we step out of that debate and  then we talk about biodiversity loss and  the crisis that is creating then we step  out of that debate and we look at the  livability of our cities and we see that  in Europe more than 400,000 people die  premature deaths because of bad air  quality etc etc then we look at our  economy and we say we have to protect  jobs so don't hurry up too much but we  are at a time when mother earth is  telling us I can't do this anymore in  this way where the population of the  earth is growing so we need to find a  solution for that and where we're in the  middle of an industrial revolution which  is going to upturn the entirety of the  world's economy so how do we bring these  things together first of all this change  is going to happen whether we like it or  not whether we do something about it or  not it's going to have so we have a  choice do we become masters of that  change do we organize it in such a way  so that we leave no one behind  or do we just let it happen and then see  what happens and one thing I know if we  study previous industrial revolutions  unchecked an industrial revolution will  create a small group of incredible  winners and a huge huge group of  incredible losers that's in the nature  of disruption caused by Industrial  Revolution so it needs to be managed and  corrected and that's why you have  governments and politics that's why you  have democracies but now with the  climate crisis coming in there and the  fact that Mother Earth can't sustain us  if we continue to live like we live we  need to combine the two things we need  to make sure that this climate crisis  doesn't lead to a few winners and the  huge amount of losers and that the  Industrial Revolution does do the same  thing at the same time so they need to  reinforce each other in the right  direction that is actually at the core  of the debate you are having here today  and that's why when we present the  European Green Deal tomorrow we want to  try and  show how sure how a holistic approach of  the issue is the only approach that it's  going to be effective looking at all the  issues that we will have to tackle and  all the measures we will have to take  because in many of the debates first of  all what is disappearing is the issue of  climate deniers I think that group is  getting smaller and smaller so that  shouldn't take up too much of our energy  anymore I'm still willing to engage with  him but they're getting you know even  Jeremy Clarkson has given up I think so  that's that's one thing secondly and  that's really worrying there's an  increasing group of people who on the  basis of science because science is  telling us almost on a weekly basis it's  even worse than we thought  while saying pardon my french was  screwed anyway never mind that to me is  the biggest threat to what we need to do  people think screwed anyway never mind  let's live it out as long as we can and  then we'll see so we have to combat that  but we can only combat that if we can  see demonstrate if we could show what we  can do and that which you can show that  people are a part of that and they could  do something themselves as well that  should be the context of I believe  presenting this the third problem I have  and I get this almost every day people  say yes we know there's a climate crisis  we know we need to do something about  this but steady on not too much think  about our industry etc etc and they  introduced a contradiction which is so  false and we have to be so careful about  this because it is my view that if we do  not if industry if our economy does not  embrace the necessity to go to that  paradigm shift and to organize it the  only thing you do is slow down decline  and  possibly postpone the inevitable but at  what cost because that's the fourth  element final element or is this  argument okay okay but we can't afford  it  you yourself are saying this will need  hundreds of billions of investment every  year true but can we afford to do  nothing  should we not and this is also a  challenge to the scientific community by  the way should we not tell people what  the costs are of not doing anything  apart from the moral costs knowing that  more than four hundred thousand people  in Europe die premature deaths because  of poor air quality apart from the cost  economically the more costs of not  acting when you know this is incredible  and I'm not even going into erratic  weather and floods and all of that and  all of that so the case should be made  that it's in our interest and it's  possible and it's economically viable to  take this transition as an opportunity  and do it and this you know already the  circular economy is proving a viable  option which creates new jobs but just  take cities for instance that's just at  the global confident of mayors where I  said that cities are are very  interesting because they they are the  place where the problems are  concentrated but also where the  creativity resides to find the solutions  so just imagine in cities you look at  our city's temperatures in many cities  in southern Europe a rising air quality  is getting lower urban transportation  getting more complicated construction is  also getting more complicated look at  nature based solutions we were proposed  tomorrow shall go into the details a  massive program of reforestation in  Europe because I believe it is necessary  sort of to do something in the different  direction than what Europe has been  doing for it for centuries but as  part of that I would like to see  greening of our cities greening the  cities makes them more liveable improves  the air quality brings a temperature  down creates carbon sink and all of that  that's a native nature based solution  that people can relate to at a very  personal individual basis this is  something we can do ourselves  immediately and I think it speaks to  sort of the very heart of our cortex you  know this is something we have a  relationship with it's part of a natural  environment look at what we can do in  the circuit economy with mace waste  management what if we can get cities to  come together to devise a plan to have a  mission free public transport it's not  that difficult it can be done but if  individual cities do this on an  individual basis they perhaps cannot  gather the strength to negotiate the  best possible deals with those who build  the infrastructure and those who provide  the electric buses etc but what if we  get cities to scale that on a way that  you don't order 100 or 200 buses but  5,000 6,000 10,000 you get a completely  different proposition and you make a  very interesting proposition to European  car industry that is looking for new  opportunities my intention today is not  to give you a blueprint our intention  tomorrow is also not to go to give you  blueprint it's an open invitation  because if governance governance public  and private has to change and it does  because of this new reality it also has  to be more open open to people bringing  other solutions and better ideas open to  a dialogue and whether this is the right  choice to make etcetera etcetera but at  the same time it has to be rigorous we  have to commit to be a climate mutual  continent by 2050 that is a rigorous  commitment and if you make that rigorous  commitment you have to think back what  does that mean for 2040 what does that  mean for 2030 which is you know 10 years  down the road it's tomorrow  and this is this rigger I believe will  offer the clarity to industry and  investors and banks on where we will go  and that will hopefully unleash the  level of investment that is needed to  scale up a climate action also through  economic means also based on nature  based solutions I strongly believe in  this approach but the rigor in saying  this is what the science says this is  what we need to do that's what I need to  be in ten years time that's what we want  to be in in a generations time that  rigor is not saying to the outside world  so this is the prescription and we will  just tell you exactly what to do step by  step no but it is the framework within  which the measures should be taken that  we can take I think corporate business  is ready for this I get clear signals  that the banking sector and the  financial sector is ready for this I  still think we have a lot of work to do  to convince small and medium sized  enterprises then they have a huge part  to play in this because they you know  given also the the economic challenges  they've had over the last ten years they  don't have the luxury to look ahead ten  or twenty years they're thinking about  tomorrow and next year  and we should be offering them some  solutions and I want to end on on a  second concrete point I already  mentioned the issue of reforestation and  greening city's second concrete point we  need to build differently it is possible  to build with different materials that  are sustainable it is also possible to  create building materials that are more  sustainable that's one side of things  but we have so many existing buildings  they also need to be brought up to a  level of energy efficiency that helps us  reduce emissions especially in cities to  do that houses will have to be isolated  solar panels will have to be installed  or heat pumps will have to be installed  for many people this is an investment  they'd like to make but they can make  they don't have that  ready so we should be thinking with  other partners and ways of empowering  smaller medium-sized enterprises to make  an offer to residents or to corporations  or to other owners of property and  saying we will do this work will isolate  your house make it energy sustainable  this might cost 1520 thousand euros we  will not ask you to pay for that  we'll ask you to sign a contract and  from the day we have done this you will  pay the same amount of money you're now  paying for energy but then to us and  then after a certain amount of time you  will own what is done to your house your  house will be more valuable will be  worth more than today and you energy  bill will go down because I hear this a  lot yes all very nice but you're asking  us to pay so much more for energy we  can't afford it so this is another  concrete proposal and I'd love to end on  that where you can say that you can  bring in the economy and smaller  medium-sized enterprises to do the right  thing  create more jobs these are jobs that  can't go anywhere because they have to  be done on the spot jobs for people  especially in the lower middle class in  the middle class who are most squeezed  in this economy and in this economic  transition and this is a way we can have  everybody engaged in a momentous  movement which I believe we cannot  afford to miss thank you very much  [Applause]

